A whole `Style` held in state, handed to a node's `style` and replaced or
written a field at a time by a handler.

On the Go targets it did not compile: the `Style` a Model field and the
`__merge_Style` helper named was a library struct no backend declared, since
only a struct a signature names was promoted, and the helper compared a
`Measurement` and a `snglcolor.Color` field against `0`. Nothing applied it
either -- fyne and bubbletea dropped a style that was not a literal, and html
wrote `setAttribute("style", <object>)` in the handlers and nothing on first
render.

A var's library struct type is now declared like a signature's, and the merge
compares each field against its own Go zero. Each target turns the value into
its own style where the program runs, reading a zero field as unset, which is
what `__merge_Style` already means by one: fyne through `__styleTheme` into
the node's theme override, bubbletea through `__styleLipgloss` on every
render, html through `_snglStyleCSS` on first render and after each write.
gtk4 applies no Style paint at all, literal or not, and here only has to
compile.

deny out/none/html/index.html `setAttribute\("style", __merge` -- the style attribute is written as CSS text, never as the struct itself
deny * `!= 0 \{\n\t\tbase\.(Color|FontSize)` -- a struct-typed field is compared against its composite zero

-- style.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

ui.window {
    var s ui.Style = {color=#ff0000, fontSize=14px}

    ui.button(text="swap", @click { s = {color=#0000ff, fontSize=20px, fontWeight=bold} })
    ui.button(text="tint", @click { s.color = #00ff00 })
    ui.text(value="styled", style=s)
}
